data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

EF Core's Azure Cosmos DB provider **removes the sync-over-async opt-in entirely**. The `EnableSynchronousAccess` option you configured in .NET 10 no longer exists. All synchronous LINQ operators (`ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, `SaveChanges()`, etc.) against the Cosmos provider will throw an `InvalidOperationException` at runtime.

## How to fix it

Convert all synchronous calls to their async equivalents:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- `ToList()` → `ToListAsync()` + `await`
- `First()` → `FirstAsync()` + `await`
- `Count()` → `CountAsync()` + `await`
- `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()` + `await`
- Return types become `Task<T>` / `Task`
- Methods renamed with `Async` suffix (convention)

The async methods require `using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore`. Note that `async` will propagate up through your call chain — callers of these methods will also need to become async.
